======================================================================
t_{j+1}/t_j (raw) = -(g - 1)*(j + m + 1)*(j + m - n)/((j + 1)*(j - n))
t_{j+1}/t_j (factored) = -(g - 1)*(j + m + 1)*(j + m - n)/((j + 1)*(j - n))

Matching against the canonical 2F1-term ratio (A+j)(B+j)z / [(C+j)(1+j)]:
canonical ratio = z*(A + j)*(B + j)/((C + j)*(j + 1))
candidate (A,B,C,z) = (-(n-m), m+1, -n, 1-g) -> ratio = -(g - 1)*(j + m + 1)*(j + m - n)/((j + 1)*(j - n))
difference vs true ratio (should be 0): 0
MATCH CONFIRMED: t_j is (up to the j=0 term t_0=C(n,m)) exactly the
  j-th term of  2F1(-(n-m), m+1; -n; 1-g).

>>> New fact (this front, PROVED by the ratio-test above):
>>>   T(n,m) = C(n,m) * 2F1(-(n-m), m+1; -n; 1-g)
>>> a TERMINATING Gauss hypergeometric series (upper parameter
>>> -(n-m) is a nonpositive integer for m<=n), genuinely different
>>> from the predecessor's diagonal 2F0 (A_k) and Charlier objects.

======================================================================
Part B: sympy finite-sum cross-check + term-by-term (n<=8)
======================================================================
[Self-caught issue -- see ATTEMPT.md Self-caught-issues section:
 an EARLIER version of this Part used sp.hyper([-(n-m),m+1],[-n],z)
 directly. At m=0 this degenerates: the upper parameter -(n-m)=-n
 EXACTLY equals the lower parameter -n, triggering the classical
 confluent identity 2F1(a,b;a;z)=(1-z)^(-b) (an INFINITE geometric
 series), not the intended finite truncation -- sympy's hyper()
 evaluates the m=0 case as the non-terminating series, silently
 giving the wrong (too-large) number. Caught immediately: ALL 8
 mismatches in that run were exactly the m=0 rows. Fixed below by
 evaluating the FINITE Pochhammer sum explicitly (unambiguous,
 no analytic-continuation subtlety) instead of sp.hyper().]

Part B: 44 (n,m) pairs checked at gamma=3/10 (finite-sum evaluator), 0 mismatches
Part B: 0 mismatches once the finite (unambiguous) evaluator is used.
Note for the record (not a further mismatch, m=0 handled correctly
above by explicit truncation at nterms=n-m): the DEGENERATE identity
2F1(-(n-m),m+1;-n;z) is only equal to sympy's hyper()-evaluated
(1-z)^-(m+1) when treated as an infinite series; as a genuinely
TERMINATING sum (truncated at j=n-m, per the combinatorial
definition) it always equals T(n,m)/C(n,m), for every m including 0.

